From b2f487d00ffe6a200cf64b22d629920bdec35081 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: josuemoraisgh <josue@ufu.br>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 14:39:35 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(esp32): wake the Core when publishing a register read

readReg() published SIM_READ and spun until the Core answered, but it was
the only synchronous request that did not signal the MCU poll doorbell.
waitForQueueDrain() had just emptied the queue, so the Core's poll thread
was normally asleep and only saw the read when its bounded wait (5 ms)
expired. Measured in the guest with GPIO_IN reads: p50 5003 us with the
default 5 ms wait, 931 us with 1 ms and 20597 us with 20 ms. The vCPU
holds the BQL for the whole wait, so every read also stalled the main
loop and the other vCPU.

Signal the doorbell after publishing the read, as publishQueueEntry() and
i2cBurstTransfer() already do.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 softmmu/simuliface.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/softmmu/simuliface.c b/softmmu/simuliface.c
index 9f03af1..63cf9d8 100644
--- a/softmmu/simuliface.c
+++ b/softmmu/simuliface.c
@@ -548,6 +548,11 @@ uint64_t readReg( uint64_t addr )
     qatomic_store_release(&m_arena->qemuAction, 0);
     m_arena->simuAction = SIM_READ;
     qatomic_store_release(&m_arena->simuTime, simuClockNs()*1000);
+    /* 2026-09-29: a leitura é o único pedido síncrono que não acordava o Core. Com a fila já
+     * vazia (waitForQueueDrain() acima), a thread de poll do Core estava dormindo e só via o
+     * SIM_READ no fim da espera limitada (5 ms): GPIO_IN levava ~5 ms por leitura, com a vCPU
+     * parada esperando. Mesmo doorbell de publishQueueEntry() e i2cBurstTransfer(). */
+    signalPollDoorbell();
 
     uint64_t timeout = 0;
     bool timedOut = false;
